      "writtenOn": "2025-06-12T19:37:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "patch_pkvm() is called twice: in apply_retpolines() and apply_returns(), for the same address range. Seems redundant?\n\nAlso apply_retpolines() and apply_returns() are called not just for the kernel image but also for modules (in module_finalize()), and it seems we don\u0027t need patch_pkvm() there?\n\nSo maybe it\u0027s better to call patch_pkvm() just once, in alternative_instructions(), after calling apply_retpolines() + apply_returns()?",
      "revId": "53d9ca603a94f30501565ddce319819dc0616ea5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a2a74a0c_91b9f1ea",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 898,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015280
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-13T04:23:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "apply_retpolines takes the section address __retpoline_sites ~ __retpoline_sites_end, and apply_returns takes the section address __return_sites ~ __return_sites_end. As they are two different ranges, calling patch_pkvm twice for different sections seems reasonable.\n\nRegarding where to call the patch_pkvm, probably we can call them inside the for loop of the apply_retpolines and apply_return. Although it will be called by module_finalize() which is not necessary for the pkvm, the overhead is just a check to see if the addr belongs to the pkvm text or not. The benefit is that, we don\u0027t need to run the for loop twice for __retpoline_sites and __return_sites, and also don\u0027t need to have another opcode checking, which sounds more attractive to me.",
      "parentUuid": "46bc99a1_667107cc",
      "revId": "53d9ca603a94f30501565ddce319819dc0616ea5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
